;_ul]:space-y-1"><p>In this post, I'll do a deep dive into the internals of=
 a modern dense transformer <a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/tr=
ansformer#ref-1" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:t=
ext-blue-800 ">[1]</a>. I'll focus exclusively on the forward pass on a sin=
gle GPU, as if we were about to perform a training step, while ignoring the=
 backward pass and distributed systems details (in practice, large Transfor=
mers are sharded across multiple devices during both training and inference=
).</p><p>As a running example, I'll use the exact architecture of <a href=
=3D"https://huggingface.co/EssentialAI/rnj-1.5-instruct" target=3D"_blank" =
rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 underline hover:text-blu=
e-800 ">Rnj 1.5</a> - a model I worked on with my team at Ashish Vaswani's =
AI Lab (Essential AI Labs).</p><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4=
" style=3D"background-color: rgb(227, 242, 253); border-left-color: rgb(33,=
 150, 243); color: rgb(13, 71, 161);"><div class=3D"flex items-center mb-2 =
font-semibold"><span class=3D"mr-2 text-lg">=F0=9F=92=A1</span>The team beh=
ind Rnj-1.5:</div><div class=3D"text-sm leading-relaxed"><p>Rnj 1.5 could n=
ot have happened without an amazing group of people (sorted alphabetically)=
:</p><p><b>Code pod:</b> Adarsh Chaluvaraju, Devaansh Gupta, Yash Jain, Som=
anshu Singla, Saurabh Srivastava (tech lead), Anil Thomas</p><p><b>STEM pod=
:</b> Aleksa Gordi=C4=87 (tech lead), Michael Pust, Tim Romanski, Ali Shehp=
er, Kurt Smith (tech lead), Ameya Velingker</p><p><b>Infra pod:</b> Mike Ca=
llahan, Philip Monk (tech lead), Khoi Nguyen (tech lead), Alok Tripathy, Ya=
sh Vanjani</p><p><b>Org:</b> Divya Mansingka, Mohit Parmar, Peter Rushton</=
p><p><b>Research and Engineering Roadmap:</b> Ashish Vaswani</p></div></div=
><p>We announced it this week, with weights released on <a href=3D"https://=
huggingface.co/EssentialAI/rnj-1.5-instruct" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noope=
ner noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 underline hover:text-blue-800 ">Hugg=
ing Face</a>.</p><p>It's a long-context follow-up to Rnj 1.0 <a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-2" rel=3D"noopener noreferre=
r" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[2]</a> that extends the co=
ntext window from 32k to 160k, scoring 79% on RULER on a 128k context windo=
w. This release also offers stronger coding abilities on a wider range of h=
arnesses. See our <a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/EssentialAI/rnj-1.5-ins=
truct" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600=
 underline hover:text-blue-800 ">model card</a> for more details.</p><p>Thi=
s post is structured into seven parts:</p><ol><li><a href=3D"https://www.al=
eksagordic.com/blog/transformer#cpt1" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"=
text-yellow-600 hover:text-yellow-800 ">Transformer forward pass</a>: high-=
level flow of a token</li><li><a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/=
transformer#cpt2" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-yellow-600 hove=
r:text-yellow-800 ">RMSNorm</a>: the normalization layer</li><li><a href=3D=
"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#cpt3" rel=3D"noopener norefe=
rrer" class=3D"text-yellow-600 hover:text-yellow-800 ">GeGLU MLP</a>: GELU-=
gated feedforward block</li><li><a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blo=
g/transformer#cpt4" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-yellow-600 ho=
ver:text-yellow-800 ">MHA</a>: multi-head self-attention</li><li><a href=3D=
"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#cpt5" rel=3D"noopener norefe=
rrer" class=3D"text-yellow-600 hover:text-yellow-800 ">YaRN</a>: positional=
 embeddings for long context</li><li><a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.co=
m/blog/transformer#cpt6" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-yellow-6=
00 hover:text-yellow-800 ">Core Attention</a>: global + block local</li><li=
><a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#cpt7" rel=3D"noop=
ener noreferrer" class=3D"text-yellow-600 hover:text-yellow-800 ">Tranforme=
r math</a>: FLOPs/token, cluster sizing, and more</li></ol><p>In a follow-u=
p post, I'll dive into conditional computation, focusing on sparse transfor=
mers (MoE).</p><h2 id=3D"cpt1" class=3D"font-semibold text-black text-xl mb=
-3 mt-6">Transformer forward pass</h2><p>As a running example, assume we sa=
mple 2 "documents" from a dataset, with:</p><p><ul><li>batch size =3D 1</li=
><li>sequence length =3D 16</li><li>document packing enabled</li></ul></p><=
p>We'll trace how a token flows through the transformer and, along the way,=
 unpack each component.</p><p>Let's start. Spend some time analyzing the fo=
llowing:</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 1: Tokenization stage" c=
lass=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.ale=
ksagordic.com/blog/transformer/flow_pre.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 te=
xt-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 1: Tokenization stage</div></div><p>We token=
ize the documents into sequences of integers, then pack the two documents i=
nto a single sequence.</p><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4" sty=
le=3D"background-color: rgb(227, 242, 253); border-left-color: rgb(33, 150,=
 243); color: rgb(13, 71, 161);"><div class=3D"text-sm leading-relaxed">For=
 the scope of this blog post, the tokenizer is a black-box component that t=
akes in text and maps it to a sequence of tokens, each represented by an in=
teger ID. In practice, tokenizers are =E2=80=9Ctrained=E2=80=9D on a separa=
te corpus of text using algorithms such as BPE, which learn a vocabulary by=
 repeatedly merging frequent character or byte sequences. Good tokenizer de=
sign has several desirable properties; for example, representing digits as =
individual tokens can help with numerical reasoning.</div></div><p>Alongsid=
e the tokens, we construct two supporting structures:</p><ul><li>inputs pos=
itions - used by the positional embedding module (YaRN)</li><li>segmentatio=
n mask - used in attention for masking</li></ul><p>This is the preprocessin=
g stage.</p><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4" style=3D"backgrou=
nd-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); border-left-color: rgb(117, 117, 117); color:=
 rgb(66, 66, 66);"><div class=3D"flex items-center mb-2 font-semibold"><spa=
n class=3D"mr-2 text-lg">=F0=9F=93=9D</span>Side note:</div><div class=3D"t=
ext-sm leading-relaxed">For efficiency reasons, the data is chunked ahead o=
f time, before training starts, and the data loader feeds these preprocesse=
d structures directly into the training loop. At that point, we never deal =
with raw strings. The (Spark) data pipelines and the data loader could easi=
ly be separate blog posts.</div></div><p>Next, we use the input tokens to i=
ndex into the embedding table.</p><p>You can think of the embedding table a=
s the vocabulary of the LLM.</p><p>This indexing operation converts our seq=
uence of integers into a sequence of 16 4096-dim <code>bf16</code> vectors:=
</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 2: Embedding stage" class=3D"w-m=
ax max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.c=
om/blog/transformer/flow_pro.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center t=
ext-sm mt-2">Figure 2: Embedding stage</div></div><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 ro=
unded-lg border-l-4" style=3D"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); border-=
left-color: rgb(117, 117, 117); color: rgb(66, 66, 66);"><div class=3D"flex=
 items-center mb-2 font-semibold"><span class=3D"mr-2 text-lg">=F0=9F=93=9D=
</span>Side note:</div><div class=3D"text-sm leading-relaxed"><p>Special to=
kens don't naturally appear during tokenization - no text maps to token IDs=
 &gt;=3D 128,000. They're injected during training (and later used at infer=
ence) to improve performance (e.g. FIM, repo packing, etc.) or to enforce s=
pecific behaviors (e.g. end of generation / turn, tool calls).</p><p>Let's =
dig into FIM <a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-3=
" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">=
[3]</a> (fill-in-the-middle) special tokens.</p><p>During (pre)training, we=
 take a document, split it into prefix, middle (infix), and suffix, and con=
struct a sequence of the form: <code>&lt;FIM_PRE&gt;</code> prefix <code>&l=
t;FIM_SUF&gt;</code> suffix <code>&lt;FIM_MID&gt;</code> middle. The model =
is trained to predict the middle given the prefix and suffix. This capabili=
ty can then be leveraged at inference time.</p><p>For example, imagine usin=
g Rnj 1.5 as an autocomplete model in your favorite IDE. Your cursor natura=
lly splits the code into a prefix and suffix, with the middle missing. By i=
nserting FIM tokens and ending with <code>&lt;FIM_MID&gt;</code>, you promp=
t the model to generate a completion for the gap. These tokens help communi=
cate intent to the model.</p><p>Tokenizer can easily be its own blog post, =
so I'll stop here.</p></div></div><p>Now we're ready to enter the first tra=
nsformer layer.</p><p>Note that all transformer layers have (almost) the sa=
me structure, so I'll explain just one. In practice, we pass through 32 suc=
h layers - you can think of it as a for loop, but in Rnj 1.5 each layer has=
 its own learnable weights.</p><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4=
" style=3D"background-color: rgb(227, 242, 253); border-left-color: rgb(33,=
 150, 243); color: rgb(13, 71, 161);"><div class=3D"text-sm leading-relaxed=
"><p>=E2=80=9Calmost=E2=80=9D because Rnj-1.5 uses both block-local and glo=
bal attention layers - the only difference is the mask. At a higher level o=
f abstraction, the statement still holds. More on that in the attention sec=
tion.</p><p>also note that some transformer implementations do weight shari=
ng or partial weight sharing between layers (there are many variations) but=
 here we're focusing on Rnj 1.5.</p></div></div><p>Let's do a forward pass =
through transformer blocks. Analyze the following carefully:</p><div class=
=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 3: Forward pass through transformer blocks" cl=
ass=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.alek=
sagordic.com/blog/transformer/flow_main.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 te=
xt-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 3: Forward pass through transformer blocks</=
div></div><p>At a high level, the block consists of four RMSNorm submodules=
, an MLP, an attention module, two residual connections, and two sum operat=
ions. The residual connections simply carry forward copies of vectors from =
earlier in the block.</p><p>Importantly, all submodules operate on individu=
al vectors, except for attention.</p><div class=3D"my-4 p-4 rounded-lg bord=
er-l-4" style=3D"background-color: rgb(227, 242, 253); border-left-color: r=
gb(33, 150, 243); color: rgb(13, 71, 161);"><div class=3D"flex items-center=
 mb-2 font-semibold"><span class=3D"mr-2 text-lg">=F0=9F=92=A1</span>Additi=
onal context:</div><div class=3D"text-sm leading-relaxed"><p>In practice, y=
ou'll find many variations of the transformer block. Design choices include=
 the placement, type and number of normalization layers, the exact MLP stru=
cture (gated vs. non-gated, the choice of gating function, etc.), residual =
connections structure (identity, Attention Residuals <a href=3D"https://www=
.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-4" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=
=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[4]</a>, etc.), and especially the =
attention module.</p><p>Broadly, attention mechanisms are either quadratic =
(e.g. MLA <a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-5" r=
el=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[5]=
</a>, scaled dot-product attn, etc.) or linear (e.g. Kimi Linear <a href=3D=
"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-6" rel=3D"noopener noref=
errer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[6]</a>) in sequence le=
ngth, each with trade-offs between modeling capacity (especially at long co=
ntext) and efficiency.</p></div></div><p>Once the vectors exit the final tr=
ansformer block, they're projected into a 128,256-dimensional space via a m=
atrix multiplication. This produces <code>logits</code>, which are converte=
d into a probability distribution via softmax. We sample from it during inf=
erence and use it in the cross-entropy loss during training.</p><div class=
=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 4: " class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-=
lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/flow_epi.=
png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 4: </div>=
</div><p>Next, let's dive into the individual sublayers. I'll go in reverse=
 order this time, which conveniently takes us from the simplest to the most=
 complex:</p><ol><li>RMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization)</li><li=
>GeGLU MLP (Multi-Layer Perceptron)</li><li>Attention (Scaled Dot-Product A=
ttention)</li></ol><h2 id=3D"cpt2" class=3D"font-semibold text-black text-x=
l mb-3 mt-6">RMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization)</h2><p>RMSNorm =
<a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-7" rel=3D"noop=
ener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[7]</a> is a =
normalization technique used to stabilize the training of deep neural netwo=
rks.</p><p>As mentioned earlier, RMSNorm operates on individual vectors, so=
 we'll focus on a single <code>bf16</code> 4096-dim vector (all others are =
processed in parallel in the same way). The output has the same shape and d=
type:</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 5: RMSNorm" class=3D"w-max =
max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/=
blog/transformer/RMSNorm.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-=
sm mt-2">Figure 5: RMSNorm</div></div><h2 id=3D"cpt3" class=3D"font-semibol=
d text-black text-xl mb-3 mt-6">GeGLU MLP (Multi-Layer Perceptron)</h2><p>T=
he MLP is a simple, pointwise feedforward neural network that is used to le=
arn the non-linear relationships between the input and output vectors.</p><=
p>Our variant is GeGLU (GELU-gated linear unit <a href=3D"https://www.aleks=
agordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-8" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"te=
xt-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[8]</a>), where the gating mechanism uses=
 GELU and takes the form <code>W2 @ GELU(W0@X)*(W1@X)</code>:</p><div class=
=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 6: GeGLU MLP" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto=
 rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/=
MLP.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 6: Ge=
GLU MLP</div></div><p>With ReLU, =E2=80=9Cgate=E2=80=9D is more literal bec=
ause the gating vector is nonnegative, so it only suppresses or scales feat=
ures. With GELU, gating values can be negative, so the gate can also invert=
 a feature's sign, which makes =E2=80=9Cgate=E2=80=9D a looser historical t=
erm.</p><h2 id=3D"cpt4" class=3D"font-semibold text-black text-xl mb-3 mt-6=
">MHA (Multi-Head Attention)</h2><p>MHA is a self-attention mechanism used =
to model relationships between different tokens in a sequence. We use a spe=
cial variant of MHA, called GQA, short for group query attention (the numbe=
r of K/V heads is reduced compared to Q heads, hence multiple queries (grou=
p) attend to the same key).</p><p>First, I'll give the high level overview =
- then we'll dig into the two most interesting components: YaRN and core at=
tention.</p><p>We start by mapping each vector independently into query, ke=
y, and value vectors. We then reshape them, normalize queries and keys, and=
 apply YaRN (which injects positional information through rotation). Next c=
omes core attention, which mixes information across positions. Finally, we =
apply a linear projection to produce the output.</p><div class=3D"my-6"><im=
g alt=3D"Figure 7: MHA - multi head attention" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-=
auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transfor=
mer/MHA.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 7=
: MHA - multi head attention</div></div><p>Let's now focus on YaRN (Yet ano=
ther RoPE extensioN).</p><h2 id=3D"cpt5" class=3D"font-semibold text-black =
text-xl mb-3 mt-6">YaRN</h2><p>YaRN <a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com=
/blog/transformer#ref-9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600=
 hover:text-blue-800 ">[9]</a> modifies RoPE <a href=3D"https://www.aleksag=
ordic.com/blog/transformer#ref-10" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"tex=
t-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 ">[10]</a> (rotary position embeddings) in a=
 clever way, that leads to better extrapolation to longer context lengths.<=
/p><p>But why do we need positional embeddings in the first place?</p><div =
class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 8: The WHY behind positional embeddings" =
class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.al=
eksagordic.com/blog/transformer/yarn_intro.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500=
 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 8: The WHY behind positional embeddings</=
div></div><p>Now that we understand the why, let's see how does RoPE work:<=
/p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 9: YaRN frequency table" class=3D=
"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagord=
ic.com/blog/transformer/yarn2.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center =
text-sm mt-2">Figure 9: YaRN frequency table</div></div><p>Here is a visual=
ization showing how different YaRN frequencies behave. Notice that our slow=
est frequency does 1 cycle every 1.088M positions!</p><div class=3D"my-6"><=
img alt=3D"Figure 10: YaRN frequencies" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto ro=
unded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/yar=
n3.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 10: Ya=
RN frequencies</div></div><p>With this we're ready to see how positional em=
beddings are injected during the forward pass:</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img =
alt=3D"Figure 11: YaRN - forward pass" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rou=
nded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/yarn=
4.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 11: YaR=
N - forward pass</div></div><p>That wraps up the YaRN forward pass.</p><p>N=
ow that we understand the mechanics of it you might still be wondering: how=
 does YaRN encode relative positional information via pairwise coordinate r=
otations of the query and key vectors, followed by a dot product?</p><div c=
lass=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 12: How does RoPE encode relative position=
al information?" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=
=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/yarn5.png"><div class=3D"=
text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 12: How does RoPE encode rel=
ative positional information?</div></div><p>And that's all there is to RoPE=
/YaRN! :)</p><h2 id=3D"cpt6" class=3D"font-semibold text-black text-xl mb-3=
 mt-6">Core Attention</h2><p>Finally, let=E2=80=99s analyze the core attent=
ion mechanism. In practice, we use FlashAttention, which deserves a separat=
e blog post (I actually wrote one back in =E2=80=9923, <a href=3D"https://g=
ordicaleksa.medium.com/eli5-flash-attention-5c44017022ad" target=3D"_blank"=
 rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 underline hover:text-bl=
ue-800 ">check it out</a> <a href=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/tran=
sformer#ref-11" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-blue-600 hover:te=
xt-blue-800 ">[11]</a>). Here, I=E2=80=99ll walk through vanilla attention.=
</p><p>Core attention is the mechanism that models relationships between to=
kens in a sequence. Take some time to analyze this:</p><div class=3D"my-6">=
<img alt=3D"Figure 13: Computing (seqlen, seqlen) matrix of attention score=
s" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www=
.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/attn1.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 t=
ext-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 13: Computing (seqlen, seqlen) matrix of at=
tention scores</div></div><p>Now if we just stopped here we'd have a situat=
ion where:</p><ol><li>tokens from document 1 could attend to tokens from do=
cument 2 (and vice versa)</li><li>token <code>i</code> could attend to toke=
n <code>i+1</code> (future token) which breaks the causality</li></ol><p>In=
 order to prevent this we need to introduce masking!</p><div class=3D"my-6"=
><img alt=3D"Figure 14: Attention masking &amp; value vector aggregation" c=
lass=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.ale=
ksagordic.com/blog/transformer/attn2.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-=
center text-sm mt-2">Figure 14: Attention masking &amp; value vector aggreg=
ation</div></div><p>Now imagine our sequence length is 32,768 instead of 16=
. For simplicity, assume a single document with no padding. What would the =
mask look like?</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 15: Hybrid attent=
ion: block local + global" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-a=
uto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/attn3.png"><div =
class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 15: Hybrid attentio=
n: block local + global</div></div><p>Here=E2=80=99s another way to visuali=
ze the layout, focusing on tokens at positions 9,000 and 10,000:</p><div cl=
ass=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 16: Hybrid attention layout" class=3D"w-max=
 max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com=
/blog/transformer/attn4.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-s=
m mt-2">Figure 16: Hybrid attention layout</div></div><p>You can see that i=
n most layers (block-local), these two tokens cannot attend to positions be=
yond 4,096. In the remaining eight layers (global), they can attend all the=
 way back to position 0.</p><h2 id=3D"cpt7" class=3D"font-semibold text-bla=
ck text-xl mb-3 mt-6">Tranformer math</h2><p>Finally, I want to briefly tou=
ch on the KV cache, as it=E2=80=99s an extremely important concept for unde=
rstanding inference. So far, we=E2=80=99ve looked at the forward pass durin=
g training.</p><p>During inference, transformers are autoregressive - we ge=
nerate one token at a time. It would be extremely inefficient to recompute =
the keys and values for all previous tokens at every step. Fortunately, the=
re=E2=80=99s no need: in a causal transformer, they remain unchanged. Inste=
ad, we compute them once and store them in a cache.</p><p>Let=E2=80=99s go =
through the basic KV cache storage requirements:</p><div class=3D"my-6"><im=
g alt=3D"Figure 17: KV cache calculation" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto =
rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/c=
alc1.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 17: =
KV cache calculation</div></div><p>Let's now also calculate how many learna=
ble parameters Rnj 1.5 has. We just need to go through the architecture and=
 account for all the learnable weights.</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"=
Figure 18: Number of learnable parameters calculation" class=3D"w-max max-w=
-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/=
transformer/calc2.png"><div class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2=
">Figure 18: Number of learnable parameters calculation</div></div><p><b>Fo=
r quick mental math</b> notice that you only need to account for 3 matrices=
 inside MLP and 4 matrices inside attention and you can ignore everything e=
lse.</p><p>Let's now calculate how much compute (FLOPs) we need per token. =
This is extremely valuable when it comes to planning cluster sizing - more =
on that after this section.</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 19: F=
LOPs/token calculation" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto=
 " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/calc3.png"><div cla=
ss=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 19: FLOPs/token calcul=
ation</div></div><p>It's worth remembering the <code>6N</code> formula. It'=
s also worth remembering the setting under which it holds (i.e. seqlen &lt;=
&lt; inner model dimension).</p><p>Finally let's see how we can use the abo=
ve formula for cluster sizing:</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 20=
: Cluster sizing calculation" class=3D"w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg m=
x-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordic.com/blog/transformer/calc4.png"><d=
iv class=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 20: Cluster sizi=
ng calculation</div></div><p>You now go to Masayoshi Son and ask for a $1B =
seed round.</p><div class=3D"my-6"><img alt=3D"Figure 21: Profit" class=3D"=
w-max max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg mx-auto " src=3D"https://www.aleksagordi=
c.com/blog/transformer/sp.png" style=3D"width: 400px; height: auto;"><div c=
lass=3D"text-gray-500 text-center text-sm mt-2">Figure 21: Profit</div></di=
v><h2 class=3D"font-semibold text-black text-xl mb-3 mt-6">Epilogue</h2><p>=
